{%- comment -%}
  ─── THE IDENTITY ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  One line of markup that makes every page of the shop stand in front of the
  same wall the products were photographed against.

  WHY A SNIPPET AND NOT JUST CSS. A Shopify asset served as `.css` is a static
  file — Liquid does not run inside it — so the stylesheet cannot write the
  URL of another asset. The texture's address therefore has to be handed to the
  stylesheet from a Liquid context, once, as a custom property. That is all this
  file does, plus pulling in the stylesheet itself so the two can never be
  installed apart.

  WHERE TO RENDER IT. Both marathon sections render it, so the home page and
  every category page carry the identity on their own. Render it from
  `layout/theme.liquid` as well — see theme/INSTALL.md — and the product page,
  the cart, the search results and the policy pages carry it too, which is the
  whole point: the environment does not stop at the pages this rebuild owns.

  RENDERING IT TWICE IS SAFE. The stylesheet link is the same URL both times
  and is fetched once; the custom property is declared with the same value both
  times. There is no flag to set and nothing to keep in sync.

  THE TEXTURE. `marathon-fence.jpg` is the owner's own photograph of the mesh,
  levelled to remove the camera's lighting falloff and cut to a tile that
  repeats without a seam (447x384, one whole number of cells in each direction,
  53 KB, greyscale). It is LIGHT — mesh on near-white, exactly as photographed.
  The stylesheet darkens it with a multiply blend rather than shipping a dark
  copy, so the depth of the wall is one number in CSS that can be tuned in the
  theme editor's own preview instead of a re-export.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'marathon-storefront.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
<style>
  :root { --mc-fence: url({{ 'marathon-fence.jpg' | asset_url }}); }
</style>
